    {
      "slug": "game-jams-teach-shipping",
      "title": "Why game jams are still the best place to learn to ship",
      "tags": [
        "developer-skills",
        "career-growth"
      ],
      "daysAgo": 2,
      "markdown": "Forty-eight hours, one theme, and a hard stop. Nothing teaches scope like a deadline that will not move.\n\n## What a jam teaches\n\n- Cut features early, not late.\n- Playtest before it is pretty.\n- Ship something, even if it is small.\n\nPlaceholder copy for a developer-skills post.",
      "excerpt": "Nothing teaches scope like a deadline that will not move.",
      "featured": true,
      "coverUrl": "/api/assets/00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006",
      "coverAlt": "Abstract placeholder artwork",
      "authors": [
        "alex-morgan"
      ],
      "commentsEnabled": true
    },
    {
      "slug": "how-researchers-choose-targets",
      "title": "How one security researcher chooses what to investigate",
      "tags": [
        "security"
      ],
      "daysAgo": 5,
      "markdown": "Good researchers do not start with tools. They start with a question: where would a mistake hurt the most?\n\n## Their checklist\n\n1. Follow the data that matters.\n2. Read the code nobody has touched in years.\n3. Look where two systems meet.\n\nPlaceholder security interview.",
      "excerpt": "Start with where a mistake would hurt most.",
      "featured": true,
      "coverUrl": "/api/assets/00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007",
      "coverAlt": "Abstract placeholder artwork",
      "authors": [
        "sam-rivera"
      ],
      "commentsEnabled": true
    },
    {
      "slug": "secret-scanning-at-scale",
      "title": "Secret protection has to scale with the software",
      "tags": [
        "security",
        "engineering"
      ],
      "daysAgo": 9,
      "markdown": "Repositories grow, and so do the credentials accidentally committed to them. Scanning once is not enough; scanning on every push is the baseline.\n\n```text\npush -> scan -> block or warn -> rotate\n```\n\nPlaceholder engineering copy.",
      "excerpt": "Scanning once is not enough. Every push is the baseline.",
      "coverUrl": "/api/assets/00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008",
      "coverAlt": "Abstract placeholder artwork",
      "authors": [
        "alex-morgan"
      ],
      "commentsEnabled": true
    },
    {
      "slug": "rebuilding-storage-for-scale",
      "title": "Rebuilding our storage layer for the next decade",
      "tags": [
        "engineering",
        "architecture-optimization"
      ],
      "daysAgo": 14,
      "markdown": "Our storage design served us for ten years. Then the number of automated commits grew faster than the number of people.\n\n## What changed\n\n- Writes are batched per repository.\n- Reads are served from replicas close to the request.\n- Large objects moved to their own tier.\n\nPlaceholder architecture deep dive.",
      "excerpt": "Ten good years, then automation outgrew the design.",
      "featured": true,
      "coverUrl": "/api/assets/00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000009",
      "coverAlt": "Abstract placeholder artwork",
      "authors": [
        "sam-rivera"
      ],
      "commentsEnabled": true
    },
    {
      "slug": "an-open-benchmark-for-code-review",
      "title": "An open benchmark for automated code review",
      "tags": [
        "ai-ml",
        "engineering"
      ],
      "daysAgo": 20,
      "markdown": "Automated reviewers are easy to demo and hard to measure. We are publishing a benchmark of real, anonymised pull requests with the comments a careful human left.\n\n## How to use it\n\nRun your reviewer, compare its comments, and report precision as well as recall. A reviewer that comments on everything is not helpful.\n\nPlaceholder research announcement.",
      "excerpt": "Easy to demo, hard to measure. A benchmark built from real reviews.",
      "coverUrl": "/api/assets/00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a",
      "coverAlt": "Abstract placeholder artwork",
      "authors": [
        "alex-morgan"
      ],
      "commentsEnabled": true
